# Third-party code in Dex

## rust-ui — `RUST-APP/crates/` and `RUST-APP/style/`

The Rust client's component library is **rust-ui** by Max Wells.

    https://github.com/rust-ui/ui
    Copyright (c) 2026 Max Wells
    MIT License — the full text is in RUST-APP/crates/registry/LICENSE.rust-ui

Vendored, not depended on: `crates/registry` (the components, hooks, blocks and
charts), `crates/icons`, `crates/leptos_ui`, `crates/tw_merge`,
`crates/autoform`, and `style/` (the Tailwind v4 theme).

### Why a copy rather than a dependency

The requirement was the exact interface — exact typography, exact icons, exact
colour scheme — and a copy is the only way to hold that still. A version bump
that restyles a button is a change to Dex's appearance arriving without a Dex
commit; vendored, the same bump is a diff someone reads.

It also removes a build-time network dependency. `RUST-APP` builds from a clean
checkout with no registry fetch for the components, which matters for a client
whose whole point is running locally.

### What was taken, and what was not

Taken: the component library and its theme.

Dropped: upstream's own site, server, Tauri shell and e2e suite — RUST-APP has
its own — and `crates/ui-cli`, the `ui add <component>` tool. Vendoring the
whole registry means there is nothing left for it to fetch.

### Changes made

Recorded in `RUST-APP/crates/registry/VENDORED.md`, with the reason for each.
At the time of writing there is one: a missing trait import in seven files that
upstream never hits because it builds them a different way.
